<h2 id="gps-global-positioning-system">GPS (Global Positioning System)</h2>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/advanced-sensors/gps-module.png"><img src="/assets/advanced-sensors/gps-module.png" alt="Top view of a round black Here4 GPS module with a triangle on top pointing forward." width="1000" height="1000" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><p>A GPS module (Here4). Mounted on top of an aircraft, it receives signals from GPS satellites; the triangle points toward the front of the aircraft.</p></figcaption></figure>
<p>GPS uses a constellation of satellites in earth orbit to determine the precise location of a receiver on the ground. Radio frequency waves are transmitted from the satellite to the receiver and the time it takes for a signal to travel between the two is measured and a distance calculation is derived. It takes at least 4 satellites to determine a 3D position (latitude, longitude, and altitude) however, in practice it takes about 11 satellites to get a very good fix on a location. Atomic clocks aboard the orbiting satellites help to make very accurate measurements of signal travel time.</p>
<h3 id="how-gps-finds-your-position">How GPS finds your position</h3>
<p>The GPS satellites orbit about 20,200 km (12,550 miles) above the earth and circle it twice a day. The United States keeps at least 24 of them working at all times, spread across six orbits so that at least four are above the horizon from almost anywhere on the planet. Each satellite constantly broadcasts its position and the exact time from its atomic clock.</p>
<p>The radio signal travels at the speed of light and takes less than a tenth of a second to reach you. Your receiver compares the time the signal was sent with the time it arrived, and that tells it how far away the satellite is. This is why the clocks matter so much: light travels about 30 cm (one foot) in a billionth of a second, so a clock that is off by just one millionth of a second puts you about 300 meters off.</p>
<p>Knowing your distance from one satellite puts you somewhere on a giant sphere around it. A second satellite narrows that down to a circle where the two spheres cross, and a third narrows it to two points, one of which is usually far out in space. So why do you need four? The satellites carry atomic clocks, but the receiver in your drone has a cheap clock that isn’t nearly accurate enough. The fourth satellite lets the receiver solve for its own clock error at the same time as its position. With more than four, the receiver can average out errors, which is why more satellites give a better fix.</p>
<h3 id="gps-glonass-galileo-and-beidou">GPS, GLONASS, Galileo, and BeiDou</h3>
<p>GPS is the United States’ system, but it is not the only one. Russia runs GLONASS, Europe runs Galileo, and China runs BeiDou. The general name for all of them is GNSS (Global Navigation Satellite System), though most people still just say GPS. Modern receivers listen to several systems at once, so they see many more satellites. The ASCEND flight controller uses a u-blox M9 receiver, which can track all four systems at the same time. With a clear view of the sky it can often see more than the 11 satellites mentioned above.</p>
<h3 id="how-accurate-is-gps">How accurate is GPS?</h3>
<p>Under open sky, a GPS-enabled smartphone is typically accurate to within about 5 meters (16 feet). The receivers in flight controllers are about as good, and often better. Accuracy gets worse near buildings, bridges, and trees.</p>
<p>GPS altitude is less accurate than its horizontal position, usually about one and a half to two times worse. All the satellites a receiver can see are above it, none below, so the geometry is much better for measuring side to side than up and down. That is why flight controllers use the pressure sensor as their main altitude source and use GPS altitude only as a check.</p>
<p>When you need better accuracy there are ways to get it. In North America, WAAS satellites send corrections that improve accuracy for receivers that can use them. For mapping and surveying, RTK (real-time kinematic) GPS uses a base station on the ground with a known position to correct the aircraft’s receiver to within a few centimeters.</p>
<h3 id="what-reduces-gps-accuracy">What reduces GPS accuracy</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>A blocked view of the sky.</strong> Buildings, trees, hills, and even the wing or the pilot’s body block signals. Mount the GPS on top of the aircraft with nothing above it, and get your fix outdoors, not in a building or a vehicle.</li>
<li><strong>Reflections (multipath).</strong> Signals that bounce off buildings or the ground arrive a little late, which makes the satellite seem farther away than it is.</li>
<li><strong>Satellite geometry.</strong> Satellites spread across the sky give a better fix than satellites bunched together. Receivers report this as dilution of precision, or HDOP for the horizontal part. Lower is better; under about 2 is good. QGroundControl shows HDOP when you click the GPS icon.</li>
<li><strong>Interference.</strong> GPS signals are extremely weak by the time they reach the ground. Video transmitters, USB 3 cables, and other electronics close to the GPS antenna can drown them out. Keep other electronics away from the GPS antenna.</li>
<li><strong>The atmosphere.</strong> The upper atmosphere slows the signals down a little. Receivers correct for most of this, but not all of it.</li>
</ul>
<h3 id="getting-a-fix">Getting a fix</h3>
<p>When a receiver starts from scratch, called a cold start, it has to find the satellites and download information about where they are. With a clear view of the sky a modern receiver usually gets a fix in about half a minute. It can take a few minutes the first time you use a new receiver, or after it has been off for a long time or moved a long distance. If it was used recently it can get a fix in a couple of seconds.</p>
<p>Wait for a 3D fix before you arm. The flight controller records your home position when you arm, and a poor fix gives you a poor home position, which is where the airplane will try to return. On the ASCEND flight controller the GPS LED shows this: off means no 3D fix, blinking means it has a fix but it is not yet good enough to arm, and solid means the GPS is ready.</p>
<h3 id="how-ardupilot-uses-gps">How ArduPilot uses GPS</h3>
<p>GPS gives the flight controller three things: where the aircraft is, how fast and in what direction it is moving, and the exact time. The speed comes from the Doppler shift of the satellite signals and is far more precise than the position, accurate to a few centimeters per second. As you read in the sensor fusion section, the EKF uses that speed to keep the attitude estimate accurate in turns.</p>
<p>Anything that needs to know where the airplane is depends on GPS: the home position, return to launch (RTL), loiter, AUTO missions, and geofences. If the GPS fails, the airplane can still be flown in modes like MANUAL and FBWA, which don’t need to know where the airplane is, but it can’t navigate on its own. That is a good reason to keep your manual flying skills sharp even when you plan to fly autonomous missions.</p>
<h2 id="airspeed-indicators">Airspeed indicators</h2>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/advanced-sensors/airspeed-sensor.png"><img src="/assets/advanced-sensors/airspeed-sensor.png" alt="An airspeed sensor: a long metal pitot-static tube with two tubing connectors at its back end, beside a small circuit board carrying a pressure sensor." width="486" height="328" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><p>An airspeed sensor: a pitot-static tube and the pressure sensor board it connects to.</p></figcaption></figure>
<p>Airspeed indicators compare ram air (air forced into a tube from forward aircraft motion) with static air (ambient air pressure outside of the aircraft in a relatively smooth area of airflow) to determine speed. An airspeed sensor is made up of a pitot tube and a static port.</p>
<h3 id="how-an-airspeed-sensor-works">How an airspeed sensor works</h3>
<p>The pitot tube points straight into the oncoming air. Air rams into the hole in its tip and stops, which raises the pressure inside the tube. Small holes in the side of the tube, the static ports, feel only the normal air pressure around the aircraft. The difference between the two is called dynamic pressure, and it depends on how fast the air is moving past the aircraft and how thick, or dense, the air is. Double the speed and the dynamic pressure goes up four times.</p>
<p>The sensor itself is a differential pressure sensor: a MEMS diaphragm like the one in the barometer, but with the pitot pressure on one side and the static pressure on the other, so it measures only the difference. That difference is very small. At 15 meters per second (about 34 mph), a typical speed for a trainer airplane, the dynamic pressure is only about 1.4 hPa. That is the same pressure change the barometer sees when you climb about 11 meters, which is why airspeed sensors need to be so sensitive and are so easily upset.</p>
<h3 id="airspeed-ground-speed-and-wind">Airspeed, ground speed, and wind</h3>
<p>An airplane flies through the air, not over the ground. The wing only cares about how fast the air is moving over it, which is the airspeed. GPS measures ground speed, how fast the aircraft is moving over the ground. The difference between the two is the wind.</p>
<p>Say your airplane flies at an airspeed of 15 m/s in a 5 m/s wind. Flying into the wind, its ground speed is only 10 m/s, and flying with the wind it is 20 m/s. The airspeed, and the lift from the wing, are the same both ways. This is why airplanes take off and land into the wind: the airplane reaches flying airspeed at a slower ground speed and needs less runway. It is also a trap for new RC pilots. After turning downwind the airplane suddenly looks fast because its ground speed went up, so the pilot pulls the throttle back, the airspeed drops, and the airplane stalls. Trust the airspeed, not how fast the airplane looks.</p>
<p>Air density matters too. Higher up, or on a hot day, the air is thinner, so the airplane has to fly faster through it to get the same dynamic pressure and the same lift. Pilots call the speed from the pitot tube indicated airspeed, and the airplane’s actual speed through the air true airspeed. True airspeed is about 2 percent higher than indicated for every 1,000 feet above sea level. The wing stalls at the same indicated airspeed no matter how high you are, which makes indicated airspeed the number the flight controller watches to keep the airplane flying.</p>
<h3 id="how-ardupilot-uses-airspeed">How ArduPilot uses airspeed</h3>
<p>In automatic modes ArduPilot uses airspeed to control the throttle and pitch together, keeping the airplane above its minimum airspeed and near its cruise airspeed while it climbs, descends, and turns. A real airspeed sensor helps most in windy conditions, slow flight, and autonomous landings.</p>
<p>ArduPilot can also fly without an airspeed sensor. It estimates the airspeed from the GPS ground speed and its own estimate of the wind, and uses throttle settings that are known to work. The standard ASCEND airplane flies this way: the firmware is set up with no airspeed sensor, and teams can add an MS4525 pitot sensor on the flight controller’s I2C header when they are ready for it. The estimate is good enough for normal flying, but it is only as good as the wind estimate, and it can’t warn you about a gust or a stall the way a real sensor can.</p>
<h3 id="setting-up-an-airspeed-sensor">Setting up an airspeed sensor</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mount the pitot tube in clean air.</strong> With the propeller on the nose, the prop wash would make the airspeed read high, so mount the pitot tube out on one wing, at least a foot from the fuselage. Point it straight into the airflow, with the side holes uncovered and out in front of the wing where the air is undisturbed.</li>
<li><strong>Connect the tubes the right way around.</strong> The pitot tube has two connectors: one for the pressure from the tip and one for the static holes. Follow your sensor’s instructions for which goes to which port, and make sure neither tube is kinked or leaking.</li>
<li><strong>Cover the pitot tube when you power up.</strong> The flight controller zeros the airspeed sensor when it starts. If the wind is blowing into the tube at that moment, it will think that is zero airspeed. Slip a loose cover over the tube before you plug in the battery and remove it before flight. Some sensors are also sensitive to light, so shade them.</li>
<li><strong>Check it before every flight.</strong> Gently blow into the pitot tube, or cup your hand over it, and watch the airspeed rise in the ground station. Blowing into it should show 6 m/s or more. If it barely moves, look for a blocked, leaking, or disconnected tube. An airspeed of exactly zero that never moves at all is also a sign of a problem.</li>
<li><strong>Calibrate it in flight.</strong> Every pitot installation is slightly different, so ArduPilot uses a ratio to convert the pressure into airspeed (the default is around 2.0). ArduPilot can work out the right ratio on its own during a calibration flight of a few minutes of circuits or loitering. The <a href="https://ardupilot.org/plane/docs/calibrating-an-airspeed-sensor.html">ArduPilot airspeed calibration guide</a> describes how.</li>
</ul>
<p>A blocked or failing airspeed sensor can make an autonomous airplane stall or overspeed, and it is hard for the flight controller to detect. Manned aircraft have the same problem: blocked pitot tubes from ice, water, or insects have caused serious accidents, which is why airliners heat their pitot tubes and why pilots check them before every flight.</p>
<h2 id="lidar-light-detection-and-ranging">LIDAR (light detection and ranging)</h2>
<p>LIDAR works by measuring the time it takes light to travel from the sensor to an object and return. LIDAR, if properly calibrated, can provide very accurate distance measurements. On an autonomous aircraft system LIDAR can be used to assist in autoland to detect distance from the ground far more accurately than GPS or a pressure sensor. LIDAR can also be used to map surfaces that a UAS flies over creating very detailed maps and 3D models.</p>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/advanced-sensors/lidar-sensor.jpg"><img src="/assets/advanced-sensors/lidar-sensor.jpg" alt="A Garmin LIDAR rangefinder: a black housing with two round lenses side by side, one to send light and one to receive it, and a bundle of colored wires." width="1052" height="1147" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><p>A LIDAR rangefinder (Garmin). One lens sends a pulse of light and the other receives its reflection.</p></figcaption></figure>
<h2 id="imus-inertial-measurement-units">IMUs (inertial measurement units)</h2>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/advanced-sensors/imu-axes.png"><img src="/assets/advanced-sensors/imu-axes.png" alt="Diagram titled “UAV Inertial Measurement Units” showing a box with three axes through it and a curved arrow around each, labeled yaw, pitch, and roll." width="1536" height="864" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><p>An IMU senses rotation about the yaw, pitch, and roll axes.</p></figcaption></figure>
<p>An IMU (Inertial Measurement Unit) uses a combination of gyroscopes and accelerometers to measure how the aircraft is rotating and accelerating. The flight controller uses those measurements to work out the aircraft’s orientation in space, also called its attitude: how far it is pitched, rolled, and yawed. This device is arguably the most important in an automated system. If the IMU fails in flight a crash is very likely.</p>
<h3 id="whats-inside-an-imu">What’s inside an IMU</h3>
<p>The IMU on a modern flight controller is a single chip only about 3 mm across. Inside the chip are tiny mechanical parts carved out of silicon, called MEMS (micro-electro-mechanical systems). These parts really do move, just by tiny amounts, and the chip measures that movement electronically.</p>
<p>A typical IMU has three accelerometers and three gyroscopes, one of each for the X, Y, and Z axes. That is why you will often see an IMU called a 6-axis or 6-DOF (six degrees of freedom) sensor. Add a three-axis magnetometer and it becomes a 9-axis sensor. The ASCEND flight controller uses an ST LSM6DSO32 6-axis IMU, with the magnetometer on a separate chip. The flight controller reads the IMU more than 800 times every second, so it always has a fresh picture of what the aircraft is doing.</p>
<h2 id="gyroscopes">Gyroscopes</h2>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/advanced-sensors/gyroscope-gimbals.png"><img src="/assets/advanced-sensors/gyroscope-gimbals.png" alt="Drawing of a gyroscope: a gold spinning disk on an axle, held inside two rings (gimbals) on a stand, against a black background." width="1280" height="960" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><p>A gyroscope. The gimbal rings let the spinning disk keep its orientation as the frame around it moves.</p></figcaption></figure>
<p>A gyroscope uses the principle of gyroscopic precession. If you ever played with a toy top as a child you were learning about gyroscopic precession even if you didn’t realize it at the time. When spinning, a gyroscope wants to resist force at a 90 degree angle to its rotation. This is why a top stays upright while spinning but wants to fall over as it slows down. In manned aircraft a mechanical gyroscope spins in the instrument panel keeping instruments such as the attitude indicator level even when the aircraft is banking in a turn. This provides horizon information to the pilot even when he/she can’t see out the window.</p>
<p>A digital gyroscope does the same job, but there is no spinning wheel inside. Instead it vibrates a tiny piece of material and measures how that vibration is disturbed when the sensor rotates. Some gyroscopes use a vibrating quartz crystal, but the gyroscopes in flight controllers, including the one on the ASCEND board, use a vibrating piece of silicon. This disturbance can be measured and calibrated and it is what we use in a UAS system for gyroscopic control.</p>
<h3 id="inside-a-mems-gyroscope">Inside a MEMS gyroscope</h3>
<p>Inside a MEMS gyroscope, a tiny silicon mass is driven back and forth thousands of times every second. As long as the chip is not rotating, the mass just moves back and forth in a straight line. When the chip rotates, the <strong>Coriolis effect</strong> pushes the moving mass sideways, at a right angle to the way it is vibrating. The faster the rotation, the bigger the sideways push. The chip measures that sideways movement the same way an accelerometer measures its proof mass (see the next section).</p>
<p>If you have ever tried to walk straight across a spinning merry-go-round you have felt the Coriolis effect. You walk in a straight line, but you feel pushed sideways, and the faster the merry-go-round spins the harder the push. The vibrating mass in the gyroscope feels the same push.</p>
<p>One important difference from the gyroscope in an airplane’s instrument panel: a MEMS gyroscope measures how <em>fast</em> the aircraft is rotating, in degrees per second, not what angle it is at. The gyroscope on the ASCEND board can measure up to 2,000 degrees per second. To know the angle, the flight controller has to add up all of those small rotations over time. That turns out to be the gyroscope’s biggest weakness, as you will see in <a href="#sensor-fusion">Putting it together: sensor fusion</a>.</p>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/advanced-sensors/toy-gyroscope.jpg"><img src="/assets/advanced-sensors/toy-gyroscope.jpg" alt="A spinning toy gyroscope balanced on the tip of its axle, its yellow-rimmed wheel blurred with motion inside a metal frame." width="889" height="1000" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><p>A mechanical toy gyroscope.</p></figcaption></figure>
<h2 id="accelerometers">Accelerometers</h2>
<p>Accelerometers measure acceleration, including the pull of gravity, usually in units of G (G-force). This force is measured in the X, Y, and Z axis and is reported as a value that can be interpreted by a flight computer. An accelerometer is useful for knowing which way is down and how hard the aircraft is accelerating in each direction. Combined, a gyroscope and accelerometer make up the core of an IMU (inertial measurement unit).</p>
<h3 id="inside-a-mems-accelerometer">Inside a MEMS accelerometer</h3>
<p>The easiest way to picture an accelerometer is a weight hanging on a spring inside a box. If you push the box forward, the weight lags behind and stretches the spring. The harder you push, the more the spring stretches. Measure how far the weight moved and you know how hard the box was accelerated.</p>
<p>A MEMS accelerometer is exactly that, shrunk down to the size of a grain of sand. A small block of silicon, called the proof mass, is held in place by tiny silicon springs. Fingers on the proof mass sit between fixed plates, and each finger and plate act as a small capacitor. When the mass moves, the gaps between the fingers and plates change, the capacitance changes, and the chip turns that change into a number the flight controller can read.</p>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/advanced-sensors/mems-accelerometer.svg"><img src="/assets/advanced-sensors/mems-accelerometer.svg" alt="Diagram of a MEMS accelerometer. A blue proof mass sits in the middle of a gray fixed frame, held on the left and right by zigzag springs. Blue fingers stick up and down from the proof mass and sit between orange fixed plates attached to the frame. An arrow along the bottom shows the frame accelerating to the right. Notes explain that the mass lags behind, the gaps between the fingers and plates change, and the chip measures the change in capacitance." width="900" height="520" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><p>Inside a MEMS accelerometer. The proof mass lags behind when the frame accelerates, changing the gaps between the fingers and the fixed plates.</p></figcaption></figure>
<p>Here is the part that surprises most students. Gravity pulls on the proof mass too, so an accelerometer sitting still on your workbench does not read zero. It reads 1 G, pointing straight up, because the springs are holding the mass up against gravity. Drop it and it reads zero while it falls. An accelerometer can’t tell the difference between gravity and the aircraft accelerating; it only feels the total of the two. That is great for finding which way is down while the aircraft sits still, and it is the reason the accelerometer needs help in flight.</p>
<p>Every accelerometer also has a limit to how much acceleration it can measure, called its range, usually somewhere between 2 and 32 G. A hard landing, a crash, or strong vibration can push it past that limit. When that happens the reading gets stuck at the maximum, which is called clipping.</p>
<h2 id="sensor-fusion">Putting it together: sensor fusion</h2>
<p>Neither the gyroscope nor the accelerometer is good enough on its own to keep an aircraft upright. Each one is good at exactly what the other is bad at.</p>
<p>The gyroscope is fast and smooth, but it only measures how fast the aircraft is rotating. To get the angle, the flight controller adds up all of those little rotations, and every tiny error gets added up too. A gyroscope that is off by just 0.1 degrees per second will be 6 degrees off after one minute and 36 degrees off after six minutes. This slow creep is called <strong>drift</strong>.</p>
<p>The accelerometer doesn’t drift. Sitting still, it always knows which way is down. But in flight it gets fooled, because it can’t tell gravity from acceleration. In a coordinated turn the airplane’s acceleration toward the center of the turn adds to gravity, and the total points straight down through the floor of the airplane, the same as in level flight. An accelerometer on its own would tell you the wings are level in a 30 degree bank. It is the same reason a passenger in an airliner can’t feel a gentle bank with their eyes closed, and why pilots flying into clouds without instruments can become disoriented.</p>
<p>The flight controller solves this by combining, or fusing, the two sensors. It trusts the gyroscope for quick changes from moment to moment and uses the accelerometer slowly, over time, to correct the gyroscope’s drift. ArduPilot does this with a mathematical tool called an Extended Kalman Filter (EKF). The EKF also blends in the GPS, the compass, the pressure sensor, and an airspeed sensor if the aircraft has one. GPS speed lets the flight controller work out how much of the accelerometer reading is really the airplane turning, which is a big part of why an airplane holds its attitude better with a good GPS fix.</p>
<h3 id="why-an-imu-cant-tell-you-where-you-are">Why an IMU can’t tell you where you are</h3>
<p>If an accelerometer measures every push the aircraft feels, couldn’t the flight controller just add them up to work out where the aircraft is? In theory yes. This is called inertial navigation, and submarines and airliners use it, but with very large, very expensive sensors. With the small sensors in a flight controller the errors grow far too fast. An accelerometer error of just 0.01 meters per second squared, about one thousandth of a G, would put the position estimate 18 meters off after one minute and almost 2 kilometers off after ten minutes. That is why the IMU tells the aircraft its attitude, and the GPS tells it where it is.</p>
<h3 id="taking-care-of-your-imu">Taking care of your IMU</h3>
<p>A few habits will keep your IMU giving the flight controller good information:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Keep the aircraft still when you power it up.</strong> Every time you plug in the battery, ArduPilot measures each gyroscope’s small offset (its reading when nothing is moving) and subtracts it from then on. It does this during the first few seconds after power-up. If you are holding the airplane or it is rocking in the wind, those offsets will be wrong. Set the airplane down and don’t touch it until the flight controller has finished starting up.</li>
<li><strong>Calibrate the accelerometers.</strong> Do the accelerometer calibration every time a flight controller is installed or replaced. ArduPilot asks for six positions in order: level, right side, left side, nose down, nose up, and on its back. Hold each position still when you confirm it. The level position needs to be accurate; the others only need to be within about 20 degrees. The <a href="/resources/flight-controller-v4-setup/">ASCEND Flight Controller V4 setup guide</a> walks through it.</li>
<li><strong>Mount the board firmly and the right way around.</strong> The flight controller assumes it is mounted in a particular direction. On the ASCEND board the sensor directions are built into the firmware, so mount the board as the setup guide shows and leave the orientation setting at its default.</li>
<li><strong>Keep vibration down.</strong> An accelerometer can’t tell vibration from real motion, so a shaking motor or unbalanced propeller looks like the aircraft is being thrown around. Balance your propellers, tighten your motor mount, and use a soft mount such as foam tape if needed. ArduPilot records vibration in its logs: levels below 30 m/s/s are normally fine, and levels above 60 m/s/s nearly always cause problems. It also counts clipping. The count should ideally stay at zero; a few during a hard landing are okay, but a number that keeps climbing through a flight means you have a vibration problem to fix.</li>
<li><strong>Watch out for big temperature changes.</strong> The readings shift slightly as the chip warms up or cools down. The EKF keeps correcting for small drift in flight, but going straight from a warm car to a cold field and powering up right away is asking for trouble. Let the aircraft sit at the field’s temperature for a few minutes first.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="magnetometers">Magnetometers</h2>
<p>A magnetometer is a digital compass. A magnetometer measures the earth&#x27;s magnetic field to generate a heading using cardinal coordinates north, south, east, west or a numbered heading 0-360 degrees. Magnetometers are negatively affected by large metal objects and need to be calibrated carefully whenever an aircraft is built or its electronics are moved. Some consumer drones ask for a calibration before every flight. GPS often provides a more accurate direction of travel, however an aircraft needs to be moving for a GPS to provide one. A magnetometer however, can provide a heading even when not moving. Most remote aircraft use both magnetometers and GPS to provide heading information.</p>
<h3 id="how-a-magnetometer-works">How a magnetometer works</h3>
<p>The earth’s magnetic field is weak. Depending on where you are on the planet it is only about 25 to 65 microtesla, roughly a hundred times weaker than the field right next to a refrigerator magnet. A magnetometer has to measure that tiny field accurately while sitting a few centimeters away from motors, batteries, and wires carrying many amps. That is why the magnetometer is the sensor most likely to give you trouble.</p>
<p>The field also doesn’t point straight north along the ground. It points north and <em>down</em> into the earth at an angle, called the dip or inclination. Across most of the United States it dips at more than 60 degrees, so most of the field is actually pointing into the ground. A flight controller’s magnetometer measures the field along all three axes, X, Y, and Z, and uses the accelerometer to work out which way is level. Then it can figure out which way north is even when the aircraft is banked or pitched. A handheld compass needle can’t do that, which is why you have to hold a hiking compass flat.</p>
<p>Most magnetometers in flight controllers are small chips that sense the field with a material whose electrical properties change in a magnetic field. Some use the Hall effect, where a magnetic field pushes the current in a conductor to one side and creates a small voltage. Others, including the MMC5983MA on the ASCEND flight controller, are magnetoresistive: the resistance of a thin metal film changes depending on the direction of the magnetic field passing through it.</p>
<h3 id="magnetic-north-and-true-north">Magnetic north and true north</h3>
<p>A compass points to magnetic north, which is not the same place as the geographic North Pole (true north). The angle between the two is called the magnetic declination, and it depends on where you are. In Montana a compass points roughly 10 to 13 degrees east of true north, while on the East Coast it points west of true north. Maps and GPS use true north, so the flight controller has to correct for declination. ArduPilot does this automatically: it has a built-in model of the earth’s magnetic field and looks up the declination from your GPS position. You don’t need to recalibrate the compass just because you are flying at a new field. You can look up the declination anywhere with <a href="https://www.ngdc.noaa.gov/geomag/calculators/magcalc.shtml">NOAA’s magnetic field calculator</a>.</p>
<h3 id="what-fools-a-magnetometer">What fools a magnetometer</h3>
<p>Anything near the magnetometer that is magnetic, or that changes the magnetic field, can make it read the wrong heading. There are two kinds of interference:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hard iron</strong> is anything that makes its own magnetic field: the magnets in motors and speakers, steel screws or tools that have become magnetized, and any wire carrying current. It adds a fixed offset to every reading, like someone holding a small magnet next to your compass.</li>
<li><strong>Soft iron</strong> is magnetic material that doesn’t make its own field but bends the earth’s field around it, such as steel brackets and screws. It stretches and squashes the readings depending on which way the aircraft is pointing.</li>
</ul>
<p>Electric current is the sneaky one. The wires between the battery, the ESC, and the motor can carry tens of amps, and every wire carrying current has a magnetic field around it. That field gets stronger as you open the throttle, so a compass that reads perfectly on the bench can be off by many degrees at full power. Some things that help:</p>
<ul>
<li>Keep the flight controller, and the magnetometer on it, as far as practical from the battery, the ESC, the motor, and the power wires. Many aircraft put the compass in the GPS module on a mast for exactly this reason. The ASCEND flight controller has its magnetometer on the board, so route the battery and motor wires away from it.</li>
<li>Twist the positive and negative power wires together. The current flows in opposite directions in the two wires, so their magnetic fields mostly cancel out.</li>
<li>Use nylon or aluminum screws near the flight controller instead of steel, and keep magnetized tools away from it.</li>
</ul>
<p>The world around you matters too. A compass will read wrong next to a car, on a metal workbench, over steel reinforcing bars in a concrete floor, or with a phone in your pocket. If your heading looks strange indoors, take the aircraft outside before you assume something is broken.</p>
<h3 id="calibrating-the-compass">Calibrating the compass</h3>
<p>Compass calibration measures the interference from your aircraft so the flight controller can subtract it. ArduPilot does this with an onboard calibration in QGroundControl or Mission Planner. You hold the aircraft in the air and slowly rotate it so that each side, the front, back, left, right, top, and bottom, points down toward the earth in turn, until the progress bars fill. From those readings the flight controller works out an offset for the hard iron and a correction for the soft iron, then asks you to reboot.</p>
<ul>
<li>Calibrate outdoors, away from cars, metal tables, computers, and phones, and ideally with a good GPS lock.</li>
<li>Calibrate after you build the aircraft and again whenever you move or replace the flight controller, the battery, the ESC, or the wiring near the compass. You don’t need to recalibrate at a new flying field.</li>
<li>Calibration can’t be done while the aircraft is armed.</li>
<li>If calibration keeps failing, or the offsets it finds are large (ArduPilot treats offsets over 600 as a sign of magnetic interference), look for something magnetic near the flight controller before you try again.</li>
</ul>
<p>The <a href="/resources/flight-controller-v4-setup/">ASCEND Flight Controller V4 setup guide</a> includes the compass calibration with the other sensor calibrations.</p>
<h3 id="compass-heading-and-gps-direction-of-travel">Compass heading and GPS direction of travel</h3>
<p>GPS doesn’t actually know which way the aircraft is pointing. It knows which way the aircraft is moving over the ground, called its track or course. Most of the time those are close, but in a crosswind an airplane flies with its nose turned partly into the wind, called crabbing, so it points one way and travels another. A magnetometer measures where the nose is pointing, and GPS measures where the aircraft is going. The flight controller uses both.</p>
<p>ArduPilot airplanes can even fly without a compass. A few seconds after takeoff, once the airplane is moving steadily, ArduPilot lines up its heading with the GPS direction of travel. That needs a good GPS with a clear view of the sky, and until it happens the flight controller doesn’t know its heading. A working compass gives the flight controller a heading before you launch, which is why most autonomous aircraft use one.</p>
<h2 id="pressure-sensors">Pressure sensors</h2>
<p>Manned aircraft have used the weight of the atmosphere to determine altitude from almost the very beginning of aviation. As you go higher above sea level the weight of the air above you gets less at a known rate. A pressure sensor is the primary tool for determining altitude. The easiest way to measure your altitude is to zero out your pressure sensor on the ground, then as you climb into the air the pressure sensor can determine how high your aircraft is above the launch location. If you want to know your altitude above sea level however, you will have to calibrate your pressure sensor to account for the current atmospheric conditions at your location. Air pressure changes all the time and temperature impacts air density as well. Airport weather stations report the altimeter correction at least once an hour, and more often when the weather is changing quickly. If your pressure sensor allows for this correction it can give a very accurate altitude.</p>
<p>The highlighted part of this METAR weather report, A3007, shows the altimeter calibration value of 30.07 inches of mercury. This value can be used to calibrate a pressure sensor to sea level.</p>
<p><code>KBZN 080356Z 16009KT 10SM CLR 21/11 <mark>A3007</mark> RMK AO2 SLP146 T02060111</code></p>
<h3 id="how-much-pressure-changes-with-height">How much pressure changes with height</h3>
<p>Pilots use a standard atmosphere as a reference: at sea level on a standard day the pressure is 29.92 inches of mercury (inHg), which is the same as 1013.25 hectopascals (hPa) or millibars (mbar), and the temperature is 15°C. Near sea level the pressure drops by about 1 inch of mercury for every 1,000 feet you climb, or about 1 hPa for every 8 meters (27 feet). The rate slowly gets smaller as you go higher because the air is thinner. Flight controllers usually work in hPa or pascals, while U.S. pilots and METARs use inches of mercury.</p>
<p>That gives you a feel for how small the changes are. Climbing from the ground to the Part 107 limit of 400 feet changes the pressure by less than half an inch of mercury, under 1.5 percent of the total. A pressure sensor for a drone has to measure those tiny changes very precisely.</p>
<h3 id="how-a-pressure-sensor-works">How a pressure sensor works</h3>
<p>The pressure sensor in a flight controller, often called a barometer, is another MEMS chip. Inside is a very thin silicon diaphragm stretched over a small sealed chamber. The outside air pushes on one side of the diaphragm, and the higher the pressure the more the diaphragm bends inward. Tiny resistors built into the diaphragm change their resistance as it bends, and the chip measures that change. Because the chamber is sealed with a known reference inside, the sensor measures absolute pressure, not just a difference.</p>
<p>The diaphragm also changes with temperature, so the sensor measures its own temperature and corrects for it. The ASCEND flight controller uses an MS5611, a popular barometer in flight controllers. It can sense a pressure change of about 0.012 mbar, which is roughly 10 cm (4 inches) of height. Its absolute accuracy is only about ±1.5 mbar, though, or about ±12 meters. In other words the MS5611 is very good at telling how much the pressure has <em>changed</em>, and not so good at telling exactly what the pressure <em>is</em>. That is exactly why we zero it on the ground before flying.</p>
<h3 id="how-ardupilot-uses-the-pressure-sensor">How ArduPilot uses the pressure sensor</h3>
<p>ArduPilot zeros the pressure sensor for you. It takes a ground reading when the flight controller starts up, and while the airplane sits disarmed with a GPS fix it keeps re-zeroing every few seconds and again when you arm. That way the altitude reads zero at the spot where you launch, even if the weather has changed while you were getting ready. Altitudes in a mission are normally measured relative to that home position. In QGroundControl this is the <strong>Rel</strong> altitude frame you choose in the plan settings.</p>
<p>The flight controller doesn’t rely on the pressure sensor alone. The EKF, the same filter that combines the IMU sensors, blends the barometer with the accelerometers and the GPS. The accelerometers respond instantly but drift, the GPS altitude doesn’t drift but is noisy, and the barometer sits in between: smooth and precise over minutes, but slowly fooled by weather. Together they give a much better altitude than any one of them on its own.</p>
<h3 id="what-fools-a-pressure-sensor">What fools a pressure sensor</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Weather.</strong> Air pressure changes as weather systems move through, sometimes by 1 hPa or more in an hour. To the sensor, that looks the same as climbing or descending about 8 meters (27 feet). On a short flight this hardly matters, but on a long flight, or a day when a front is coming through, the altitude can be off by several meters by the time you land. Manned pilots learn this as “high to low, look out below”: flying from high pressure into low pressure without resetting the altimeter means it reads higher than you really are.</li>
<li><strong>Airflow.</strong> Wind, prop wash, and air rushing through the fuselage change the pressure around the sensor, so the altitude can jump when the airplane speeds up or the throttle changes. Mount the flight controller where it is sheltered from moving air, and cover the barometer with a small piece of open-cell foam. The foam lets the air pressure through but slows down gusts.</li>
<li><strong>Light.</strong> Some barometers, including the MS5611, are sensitive to bright light shining directly on the sensor. The same piece of foam, or an enclosed fuselage, keeps the sun off it.</li>
<li><strong>Temperature.</strong> The sensor corrects for its own temperature, but a sudden change, such as direct sun heating the board or a cold breeze through an open hatch, can still make the reading drift. Keep the flight controller shaded and let the aircraft settle to the outdoor temperature before you power it up.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="artificial-intelligence">Artificial intelligence</h2>
<p>Trying to keep pace with the changes in (AI) Artificial Intelligence is very difficult with the speed at which it is progressing. We already use AI in many facets of our lives to save time, innovate, and solve problems. The integration with AI and robotics, particularly drones will likely improve the safety and reliability of automated systems and streamline the processing of data to near real time. One of the biggest limitations of drones has been the ability to amass large amounts of data but a lack of resources to process and make meaning from that data. AI will likely decrease data processing time and allow automated systems to operate in ways we have yet to imagine. As with any new and emerging technology there will be failures and setbacks as systems are refined and problems are solved. This course will likely not touch on AI other than to look at new and emerging AI solutions as they relate to drones. Here are a few things to look for:</p>
<ul><li>
AI can be used for real-time image processing, which could allow drones to operate in areas without GPS such as indoor environments or areas where GPS has been jammed or denied in some way.
</li><li>
Swarm drones, such as those seen in drone light demonstrations. AI can help to coordinate and generate much more complex movements of large groups of drones.
</li><li>
Remote sensing platforms for wildfire detection, crop inspection, search and rescue and other applications could allow for rapid detection of desired targets (lost hikers, wildfire, crop damage) without the need for humans to manually examine images.
</li><li>
Drone warfare is already happening between Russia and Ukraine. As each side develops new and deadlier drones integrating AI, the dark side of AI development is becoming rapidly apparent.
</li></ul>
